[{"id":"71f719d2","n":1,"exam":["final"],"year":441,"years":[441],"rep":1,"appears":"","stem":"A patient presents with pain, numbness, and tingling in the first three fingers (thumb, index, and middle finger). On examination: • Weakness in thumb opposition is noted. • Positive Tinel’s sign Most Likely Diagnosis?","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"Carpal tunnel syndrome"},{"k":"B","t":"Cubital tunnel syndrome"},{"k":"C","t":"Ulnar Tunnel Syndrome"}],"ans":"A","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"The source splits this single clinical case between Q9 and Q10; the stem and choices are joined here.","img":[],"expl":"Numbness of the radial 3½ digits, weak thenar function and a positive Tinel's sign at the wrist = median nerve compression in the carpal tunnel.","why":{"A":"Correct.","B":"Cubital tunnel gives ulnar 1½-digit numbness and weak intrinsics.","C":"Ulnar tunnel (Guyon's canal) also affects the ulnar fingers."}},{"id":"b6e2ab7b","n":2,"exam":["final"],"year":439,"years":[439,438,437,432,431],"rep":7,"appears":"Final: 439, 438, 437, 432, 431","stem":"A motor vehicle driver fell on his arm and he sustained a fracture to his humerus. He cannot extend his fingers or his wrist, which of the following nerves are affected?","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"Radial nerve"},{"k":"B","t":"Median nerve"},{"k":"C","t":"Ulnar nerve"},{"k":"D","t":"Suprascapular nerve"}],"ans":"A","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"(Hand) Source note (Final 438 A Q36): A 20-year-old male sustained a fracture of the shaft of the humerus. Which of the following will be present in examination? A. Wrist drop A (hand) due to radial nerve injury","img":[],"expl":"The radial nerve runs in the spiral groove of the humerus; a shaft fracture injures it → can't extend the wrist or fingers (wrist drop).","why":{"A":"Correct.","B":"Median injury affects flexion, thumb abduction/opposition.","C":"Ulnar injury gives claw hand and weak intrinsics.","D":"Suprascapular nerve affects shoulder abduction/external rotation."}},{"id":"c0acabe4","n":3,"exam":["final","mid"],"year":439,"years":[439,437,428,427],"rep":5,"appears":"Midterm: 427 | Final: 439, 437, 428, 427","stem":"Case scenario about a patient that came and the question clearly says \"Can Not make [OK] Sign\" what is the nerve affected ?","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"Ulnar nerve"},{"k":"B","t":"Radial Nerve"},{"k":"C","t":"Anterior Interosseous nerve"},{"k":"D","t":"Posterior Interosseous nerve"}],"ans":"C","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"( Hand )","img":[],"expl":"The anterior interosseous nerve (median branch) supplies FPL and the index FDP — without them the thumb IP and index DIP can't flex, so the patient can't make the \"OK\" sign. Pure motor, no sensory loss.","why":{"A":"Ulnar injury gives clawing and a positive Froment's test.","B":"Radial injury gives wrist drop.","C":"Correct.","D":"PIN injury gives finger drop."}},{"id":"d7a6d8eb","n":4,"exam":["final"],"year":438,"years":[438,432,431,429],"rep":6,"appears":"Final: 438, 432, 431, 429, Year not stated","stem":"After shoulder reduction the patient lost the sensation over the lateral aspect of the arm and full arm abduction, what nerve is affected?","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"axillary"},{"k":"B","t":"musclucotenes"},{"k":"C","t":"medein"},{"k":"D","t":"ulner"}],"ans":"A","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"(Hand) 438F","img":[],"expl":"Shoulder dislocation/reduction stretches the axillary nerve → deltoid weakness (loss of abduction) and numbness over the lateral upper arm (\"regimental badge\" area).","why":{"A":"Correct.","B":"Musculocutaneous injury weakens elbow flexion and numbs the lateral forearm.","C":"Median nerve doesn't supply shoulder abduction.","D":"Ulnar nerve doesn't supply shoulder abduction."}},{"id":"6c77f028","n":5,"exam":["final"],"year":438,"years":[438,436],"rep":4,"appears":"Final: 438, 436","stem":"Pt with claw hand and hx of K-wire insertion in the elbow, which of the following nerves is injured?","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"Median"},{"k":"B","t":"Ulnar"},{"k":"C","t":"Radial"},{"k":"D","t":"Musculocutaneous"}],"ans":"B","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"( hand )* Source note (Final 436 A Q39): Answer: C, Claw hand when attempt of extension (hand) C. Ulnar","img":[],"expl":"The ulnar nerve lies behind the medial epicondyle — a K-wire placed medially at the elbow (supracondylar fracture fixation) can injure it → claw hand.","why":{"A":"Median injury gives the \"benediction\"/ape hand, not claw.","B":"Correct.","C":"Radial injury gives wrist drop.","D":"Musculocutaneous injury affects elbow flexion."}},{"id":"8fa7e436","n":6,"exam":["final"],"year":438,"years":[438],"rep":2,"appears":"Final: 438","stem":"What is the part responsible for middle finger sensation in Brachial plexus?","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"C5"},{"k":"B","t":"C6"},{"k":"C","t":"C7"},{"k":"D","t":"T1"}],"ans":"C","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"( hand ) middle finger sensation innervated by median nerve Source note (Final 438 A Q48): The related trauma recall gives: \"A. Fraction of C7\". A (hand) middle finger sensation innervated by median nerve and comes from c7 brachial plexus","img":[],"expl":"Dermatomes of the hand: thumb = C6, middle finger = C7, little finger = C8.","why":{"A":"C5 supplies the lateral arm (regimental badge area).","B":"C6 supplies the lateral forearm and thumb.","C":"Correct.","D":"T1 supplies the medial forearm/arm."}},{"id":"385599f1","n":7,"exam":["final"],"year":438,"years":[438,429],"rep":2,"appears":"Final: 438, 429","stem":"A patient fell on his hand and had a fracture of the head of the radius. He is unable to extend the digits. However, he is able to extend the wrist and elbow. Hand surgery exam showed no sensory deficits. Which nerve injury most likely this patient had?","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"Axillary nerve injury"},{"k":"B","t":"Musculocutaneous nerve injury"},{"k":"C","t":"Anterior interosseаous nerve injury"},{"k":"D","t":"Posterior interosseаous nerve injury"}],"ans":"D","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"(branch from Radial nerve) ( Hand )","img":[],"expl":"Radial head fractures can injure the posterior interosseous nerve as it winds round the radial neck → finger and thumb extension lost, but wrist extension (ECRL, supplied above) and elbow extension kept; no sensory loss (pure motor).","why":{"A":"Axillary affects the shoulder.","B":"Musculocutaneous affects elbow flexion.","C":"AIN affects thumb/index flexion (OK sign).","D":"Correct."}},{"id":"4bf6e493","n":8,"exam":["final"],"year":437,"years":[437,432,431],"rep":4,"appears":"Final: 437, 432, 431, Year not stated","stem":"A 20-year-old female attempted suicide by cutting her wrist. She sustained complete transection of the median nerve. Which of the following is the expected functional loss in the hand?","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"Abduct the middle finger"},{"k":"B","t":"Abduct the thumbs"},{"k":"C","t":"Thumb opposition"},{"k":"D","t":"Abduct index finger"}],"ans":"C","ansAdded":true,"srcNote":"","img":[],"expl":"Complete median transection at the wrist paralyses the thenar muscles. Opposition (opponens pollicis) is completely lost; thumb abduction is weakened but partly kept by abductor pollicis longus (radial nerve). The source says both B and C are median but chose C.","why":{"A":"Middle finger abduction is by the interossei (ulnar).","B":"Weakened (abductor pollicis brevis), but not completely lost.","C":"Correct.","D":"Index abduction is by the first dorsal interosseous (ulnar)."}},{"id":"e60f103b","n":9,"exam":["final"],"year":437,"years":[437],"rep":1,"appears":"","stem":"a patient presented with shaft of metacarpal bones fracture after trauma, the doctor decided conservative management. what's the ideal position for splitting?","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"MCP 60° flexion, wrist neutral"},{"k":"B","t":"PIP neutral, MCP flexion 60°, wrist 30° extension"},{"k":"C","t":"Pip fully extended, MCP neutral, wrist 30° extension."},{"k":"D","t":"Pip 90°, MCP neutral, wrist neutral"}],"ans":"B","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"(Hand)","img":[],"expl":"Hand fractures are splinted in the safe (intrinsic-plus) position — wrist ~30° extension, MCP joints flexed 60–90°, IP joints straight — keeping the collateral ligaments stretched so the joints don't stiffen.","why":{"A":"Leaves the IP joints unspecified and the wrist neutral.","B":"Correct.","C":"MCP joints in extension stiffen (collateral ligaments shorten).","D":"Flexed PIPs develop flexion contractures."}},{"id":"96297b74","n":10,"exam":["final"],"year":437,"years":[437],"rep":1,"appears":"","stem":"A young patient after a hyperextension injury to the neck presented with C5 and C6 roots injury. What is the sensory loss?","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"volar aspect of hand"},{"k":"B","t":"superior deltoid"},{"k":"C","t":"lateral aspect of arm and forearm"},{"k":"D","t":"medial ??"}],"ans":"C","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"(localization of lesions within CNS) Choice D is incomplete in the source.","img":[],"expl":"C5 supplies the lateral arm and C6 the lateral forearm and thumb → loss over the lateral aspect of the arm and forearm.","why":{"A":"The volar hand is mainly C6–C8 (median/ulnar).","B":"The \"superior deltoid\" alone is only part of C5.","C":"Correct.","D":"The medial side is C8–T1."}},{"id":"9489e6b5","n":11,"exam":["final"],"year":437,"years":[437],"rep":1,"appears":"","stem":"A patient came to the ER with open fracture of fourth and fifth metacarpal bone because of falling on a sharp object. What is management?","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"External fixation and K-wire"},{"k":"B","t":"Splint"}],"ans":"A","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"“not sure” Only two answer choices are provided in the source.","img":[],"expl":"Open hand fractures need operative management — debridement/washout, antibiotics and fixation (K-wires or external fixation) — not a simple splint.","why":{"A":"Correct.","B":"A splint alone doesn't treat an open fracture."}},{"id":"301a1b11","n":12,"exam":["final"],"year":437,"years":[437],"rep":1,"appears":"","stem":"Scenario of a closed hand fracture of 5th metacarpal. with scissoring of the 4th and 5th digit and 55 degrees of angulation. What is the best management option?","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"Irrigate the wound in ER , reduce the fracture and use splint"},{"k":"B","t":"Call hand surgeon and book the OR"}],"ans":"B","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"(hand) Only two answer choices are provided in the source.","img":[],"expl":"Rotation/scissoring is one of the four indications for operative fixation (with multiple fractures, instability and comminution) → hand surgeon and theatre.","why":{"A":"The fracture is closed, and a scissoring fracture won't hold in a splint.","B":"Correct."}},{"id":"5c4e5307","n":13,"exam":["final"],"year":437,"years":[437],"rep":1,"appears":"","stem":"Pic of hand injury (laceration in the DIP) , this patient can’t flex his finger, what is the injured structure?","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"Flexor digitorum profundus"},{"k":"B","t":"Flexor digitorum superficialis"}],"ans":"A","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"(hand) Only two answer choices are provided in the source.","img":["data:image/jpeg;base64,"],"expl":"Only FDP reaches the distal phalanx, so a laceration at the DIP level that stops fingertip flexion has cut FDP.","why":{"A":"Correct.","B":"FDS inserts on the middle phalanx and flexes the PIP."}},{"id":"9b8fb906","n":14,"exam":["final"],"year":436,"years":[436],"rep":1,"appears":"","stem":"A 20-year-old male had a minor needle prick to his right index finger. Over the next two days, he developed progressive swelling, redness, and pain along the finger. He presented with the finger in a flexed position, Passive finger extension showed severe pain. What is the diagnosis?","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"Candida tendon infection"},{"k":"B","t":"Septic tenosynovitis"},{"k":"C","t":"Herpetic felon"},{"k":"D","t":"Septic felon"}],"ans":"B","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"","img":[],"expl":"A penetrating finger injury followed by Kanavel's signs — flexed posture, fusiform swelling, sheath tenderness and severe pain on passive extension — is septic flexor tenosynovitis → IV antibiotics and urgent sheath washout.","why":{"A":"Fungal tendon infection is rare and indolent.","B":"Correct.","C":"Herpetic whitlow is vesicular, at the fingertip.","D":"A felon is a fingertip pulp abscess, not the whole finger."}},{"id":"d6b2fa67","n":15,"exam":["final"],"year":436,"years":[436],"rep":1,"appears":"","stem":"A 30-year-old fell on an outstretched hand and fractured his hand near the snuffbox. Which carpal bone is most likely fractured?","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"Capitate"},{"k":"B","t":"Hamate"},{"k":"C","t":"Lunate"},{"k":"D","t":"Scaphoid"}],"ans":"D","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"(hand)","img":[],"expl":"A fall on an outstretched hand with snuffbox tenderness = scaphoid fracture, the commonest carpal fracture.","why":{"A":"Capitate fractures are rare.","B":"Hamate fractures follow club/racquet injuries.","C":"Lunate injuries are dislocations more than fractures.","D":"Correct."}},{"id":"95644106","n":16,"exam":["final"],"year":435,"years":[435,434,433],"rep":3,"appears":"Final: 435, 434, 433","stem":"A 30-year-old man who repairs electrical lines for a utility company is brought to the emergency department for the treatment of an electrical injury to the right upper extremity. Examination shows limited full-thickness burns of the flexor creases of the palm, wrist, and elbow. Doppler flowmetry shows patent palmar arch pulses. Passive motion of the fingers produces severe pain. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial step in management?","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"Burn excision and grafting"},{"k":"B","t":"Continued observation"},{"k":"C","t":"Escharotomy"},{"k":"D","t":"Fasciotomy"}],"ans":"D","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"","img":[],"expl":"Keyed fasciotomy: high-voltage injury destroys deep muscle (skin burns underestimate it) → compartment syndrome → fasciotomy (± debridement), even with palpable pulses.","why":{"A":"Grafting comes later.","B":"Observation risks muscle necrosis.","C":"Escharotomy only releases the skin.","D":"Correct (source key)."}},{"id":"00cc30c3","n":17,"exam":["final"],"year":435,"years":[435,434,433],"rep":3,"appears":"Final: 435, 434, 433","stem":"24 year old woman, presented to the clinic complaining of a mass at the dorsum of the right hand which is associated with slight pain during wrist flexion. It appeared suddenly 6 months back and is increasing in size. On examination the mass measures 2 x 2cm with normal overlying skin, has got a smooth surface, hard in consistency and is immobile. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"Squamous cell carcinoma"},{"k":"B","t":"Basal cell carcinoma"},{"k":"C","t":"Giant cell tumor"},{"k":"D","t":"Ganglion"}],"ans":"D","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"(hand)","img":[],"expl":"A smooth, firm-to-hard, fixed lump on the dorsum of the hand/wrist in a young woman, painful on wrist movement = ganglion (from the joint capsule or tendon sheath).","why":{"A":"SCC is a skin ulcer, and the skin here is normal.","B":"BCC is a skin lesion.","C":"Giant cell tumour of tendon sheath is usually on the volar fingers.","D":"Correct."}},{"id":"18bdabbb","n":18,"exam":["final"],"year":434,"years":[434,433],"rep":2,"appears":"Final: 434, 433","stem":"A patient came complaining of upper limb weakness and numbness for 2 months. He had weak shoulder abduction and numbness over the lateral arm and weakened biceps reflex. Which nerve root is likely affected?","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"C4."},{"k":"B","t":"C5."},{"k":"C","t":"C6."},{"k":"D","t":"C7."}],"ans":"B","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"(Hand)","img":[],"expl":"Shoulder abduction (deltoid), numbness over the lateral arm and the biceps reflex are all C5.","why":{"A":"C4 supplies the cape (top of the shoulder) and the diaphragm.","B":"Correct.","C":"C6 is elbow flexion/wrist extension, brachioradialis reflex, and the thumb.","D":"C7 is the triceps and the middle finger."}},{"id":"dfa84f61","n":19,"exam":["final"],"year":432,"years":[432,431],"rep":2,"appears":"Final: 432, 431","stem":"A 20 year old female attempted suicide by cutting her wrist. She sustained complete transection of the median nerve. which of the following is the expected functional loss in the hand? “Repeated Q”","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"Abduction of the thumb"},{"k":"B","t":"Abduction of the index finger"},{"k":"C","t":"Abduction of the middle finger"},{"k":"D","t":"Abduction of the ring finger"}],"ans":"A","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"(Hand) - Loss of thumb abduction and opposition","img":[],"expl":"Median nerve transection at the wrist paralyses abductor pollicis brevis → loss of thumb abduction (and opposition). Finger abduction is by the ulnar-supplied interossei.","why":{"A":"Correct.","B":"Ulnar (first dorsal interosseous).","C":"Ulnar (interossei).","D":"Ulnar (interossei)."}},{"id":"ebba902f","n":20,"exam":["final"],"year":429,"years":[429],"rep":1,"appears":"","stem":"A newborn presented with “ waiter’s tip posture’. This indicates which of the Following?","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"Axillary nerve injury from shoulder injury."},{"k":"B","t":"Erb’s palsy."},{"k":"C","t":"Klumpke’s palsy."},{"k":"D","t":"Total limb palsy."}],"ans":"B","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"(Hand)","img":[],"expl":"Waiter's tip posture (arm adducted and internally rotated, elbow extended, forearm pronated) = Erb's palsy, upper trunk (C5–C6) birth injury.","why":{"A":"Axillary injury causes deltoid weakness only.","B":"Correct.","C":"Klumpke's (C8–T1) gives claw hand.","D":"Total palsy gives a flail arm."}}]